We interpret a^{-1} and (a-1)^{-1} to exist only when gcd(a,20)=1 and gcd(a-1,20)=1 respectively. So any a counted must satisfy both gcd(a,20)=gcd(a-1,20)=1.

If gcd(a,20)=1 then a is odd (so not divisible by 2) and not divisible by 5. But then a−1 is even, so gcd(a−1,20) is at least 2, hence cannot be 1. Thus there is no integer a for which both a and a−1 are invertible modulo 20. Therefore the expression a(a-1)^{-1} ≡ 4a^{-1} (mod 20) cannot even be formed for any a in 0 ≤ a < 100, so there are 0 such integers.

Answer: \boxed{0}

